I have data from an L2 oral task that has elicited two types of pronouns 
X and Y -plus
some other category which is irrelevant to the study- from two L1s. In a 
table I have reported counts and % proportion for each type of pronoun + 
other category over the total amount of possible contexts produced
(total x + total y + total z= grand total contexts).

The objective is to verify whether the two groups differ in use of the 
two types pronoun, x and y.
Crawley (2012, p.590) suggests modelling proportion data as lmer 
following a binomial distribution ('family' function in R) since 5 
proportions vary between 0 and 100%. There are of course advantages and 
disadvantages
to this method compared to say counts or binary responses, but 
ultimately I need to cope with the fact I
have counts, and not items as in experimental tasks, which has 
consequences for the model assumed.

Following this line of reasoning Crawley suggests the following measures

y<-cbind(dead,alive) # the proportions for the two types of pronoun are 
binded under 1 column I label 'response'

model<-lmer(y~block+treatment+(1block/treatment),binomial,data=data)

First note, nowadays a binomial family analysis needs to be carried out 
in glmer and not lmer.

I decide to go down this route, plot my data with a bwplot and remove 
outliers. One problem with the data is there are several 0%s but these 
are genuine 0s (and not NAs) because subjects had a chance to produce 
pronouns but opted to produce only the second type or the other 
category, or a mix of both, instead. If my understanding is correct, 
then, I can continue with the binomial distribution fit.

My 'response' variable includes the proportions for the two pronoun 
types only but not the other category because these are not important. 
So here is a first question: does the fact the proportion of the two 
pronouns not add up to 100% make a difference to statistical analysis? I 
assume not.

After cbind on the proportion of the two pronoun types (outcome 
