Hi Edith,

it depends a lot who you're teaching.  Baayen's book is good for people
with a decent background in stats, a good linguistic vocabulary, and no
fear of programming; Field's book is OK, I guess, but really seems to be
aimed at replicating SPSS in R (and there are some weird decisions, not
least the recommendation of R Commander over R Studio!).

We've tried both (and homebrew) for Masters-level courses at Edinburgh;
we've been teaching R for about 5 years now, each year to ~70 students
from highly diverse backgrounds.  Neither book has worked very well.
Many of our students don't seem to have the right background for Baayen,
and find it intimidating; those with Psych/SPSS backgrounds can't work
out why you'd want to do SPSS "the hard way" in Field, and it has a
mixed reception from the rest, not least because we have to tell them to
ignore quite a bit of his advice.

We've decided that the only sensible way to teach our MSc students is to
run a stats course which starts (nearly) from scratch, allowing us to
ensure that they speak a common statistical language, and to introduce
core concepts and R alongside each other. If students' foundations in
regression etc. are good, it's relatively easy for them to grasp
multilevel modelling.

Luckily, there's a pretty good book, if this is the way you choose to
go: Daniel Navarro's (free!) "Learning Statistics with R",
http://health.adelaide.edu.au/psychology/ccs/teaching/lsr/.  It doesn't
get as far as repeated measures (let alone mixed models), but it
provides solid and in-depth foundations in statistics and in R.  I think
it's one of the better books I've seen; maybe it will suit your purposes.

--MC

