I second/third both Martin's points about Baayen's book (which I really
like, but it's not for beginners, unlike they are the type of person who
sits down and learns a programming language) and Alex's enthusiasm about
Gelman and Hill. It's a very accessible introduction to regression and
multilevel modeling (through R, though not primarily).

I think Shravan's new textbook is worth a look (
http://www.amazon.com/The-Foundations-Statistics-Simulation-based-Approach/dp/3642163122).
I worked with earlier versions and liked them -- provided you have an
audience that wants to get an intuitive understanding of the underlying
mathematical concepts, rather than primarily learning to apply models (I
think both are fair goals, depending on your priorities).

For some of the more complex concepts, Roger Levy has a collection of
lecture notes (perhaps in press, Roger?) that I find very illuminating. In
case it's useful, I also have a bunch of lecture slides on regression and
mixed models created in knitr (with RStudio), i.e., where the code shown on
the slides is the one that created the slides. There's further slides by
Maureen Gillespie (on coding) and Dave Kleinschmidt (on plyr, data
reshaping, and simulation-based approaches to e.g., Type I error analysis).
Let me know. Happy to share.

Florian


