Hi Frank,

If I'm seeing this right, one possibility is fitting a glm(), like Francesco suggested.

resp <- cbind(resp.x, resp.y)
m.glm <- glm(resp ~ L1, data = yourdata, family = binomial)

But glm() can take as response a "two-column matrix with the columns giving the numbers of successes and failures".
So you use cbind() on the counts, not on the proportions.

If you fit a mixed-effects model, then you'll use glmer().
Possible responses should be 0 and 1, and you model all the data points, for each participant.

m.glmer <- glmer(resp.bin ~ L1 + (1|Participant), family=binomial)

I suppose the L1 factor is a between-subjects variable, so it isn't included as a by-participant adjustment.
But another random intercept can be Item, for every item in you task (with possibly a by-item random slope):
m.glmer.i <- glmer(resp.bin ~ L1 + (1|Participant) + (1+L1|Item), family=binomial)

(note that, to simplify, I'm ignoring the third type of response here...
but one solution is fitting a model to x responses vs. all others;
and then do the same for y responses vs. all others)

Hope this helps,
João

